WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.453
When visible reasoning helps, and when it hurts.

2
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.083
Reasoning, visible or hidden, costs tokens and time.

3
00:00:06.363 --> 00:00:13.595
It earns that cost on multi-step problems: calculations, planning, reconciling conflicting sources, debugging.

4
00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:25.544
For a simple lookup or a short classification, it mostly adds latency, which is why the docs recommend low effort for simple, high-volume work, and why the right level is something to measure.

5
00:00:25.824 --> 00:00:29.579
Asking for reasoning in the visible answer has its own trade-offs.

6
00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:43.512
It helps when you want an audit trail a reviewer can read, or when the reasoning is itself the deliverable, such as quoting the evidence before giving a verdict (the quote-first pattern from lesson 4, which you can check with a string search).

7
00:00:43.792 --> 00:00:51.963
It hurts when code has to parse the output, since prose mixed into an answer breaks parsers unless you fence the final answer in tags.

8
00:00:52.243 --> 00:00:53.459
And it can mislead.

9
00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:55.381
That last point deserves care.

10
00:00:55.661 --> 00:01:12.451
Turpin and colleagues (2023) showed that chain-of-thought explanations can systematically misrepresent why a model gave its answer: when they biased models toward an answer (for example, by making the correct option always “(A)” in the examples),

11
00:01:12.731 --> 00:01:17.595
the models changed answers and then wrote explanations that never mentioned the bias.

12
00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:29.288
Anthropic’s 2025 research on its own reasoning models found the same kind of problem: models often used a hint planted in the prompt without saying so in their reasoning.

13
00:01:29.568 --> 00:01:35.477
The engineering conclusion: treat written reasoning as useful evidence for debugging, not as proof.

14
00:01:35.757 --> 00:01:37.379
Verify the answer itself.
